        <body>
  <h2>Student Rights & Discipline in District 10 (NYC): A Parent's Guide</h2>

  <p>District 10, encompassing Riverdale, Kingsbridge, Norwood, Fordham, and Bedford Park, is committed to ensuring a safe and supportive learning environment for all students. This commitment extends to upholding student rights and implementing fair and equitable disciplinary practices, especially for students with disabilities. This guide will help you navigate the disciplinary process in District 10, focusing on Manifestation Determination Reviews, suspensions, and your due process rights under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Education Law.</p>

  <h3>Understanding Discipline for Students with IEPs and 504 Plans</h3>

    <p>Students with IEPs and 504 Plans have additional protections when facing disciplinary action. The key principle is that discipline should not stem from the disability itself or from the school's failure to properly implement the student's plan. These protections are outlined in IDEA and further clarified in New York State regulations Part 200.</p>

  <h3>Manifestation Determination Review (MDR): A Critical Safeguard</h3>
  <p>A Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) is a critical process triggered when a student with an IEP (Individualized Education Program) or 504 plan faces suspension exceeding 10 school days (whether consecutive or cumulative within a school year), expulsion, or any other disciplinary change in placement.  The IEP team, including parents/guardians, relevant school personnel (including those who observed the behavior and those knowledgeable about the student's disability), convenes to determine if the student's behavior was caused by, or had a direct and substantial relationship to, their disability, or if the behavior was a direct result of the school's failure to implement the IEP or 504 plan. See 34 CFR § 300.530.</p>

  <p><b>Key Questions the MDR Team Must Address:</b></p>
  <ul>
      <li>Was the conduct in question caused by, or did it have a direct and substantial relationship to, the child's disability?</li>
      <li>Was the conduct in question the direct result of the school district's failure to implement the IEP?</li>
  </ul>

    <p><b>What Happens if the Behavior is a "Manifestation"?</b> If the team determines that the behavior *is* a manifestation of the student's disability, or was a direct result of the school's failure to implement the IEP, the behavior is deemed a "manifestation."  This means:</p>
  <ul>
      <li>The student generally cannot be disciplined in the same way as a student without a disability.</li>
      <li>The IEP team MUST conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA), unless one has already been conducted, and implement a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) for the student.</li>
      <li>If a BIP already exists, the team must review and modify it, as necessary, to address the behavior.</li>
      <li>The student must be returned to their original placement, unless the parent and the school district agree to a change of placement as part of the modification of the BIP. There are limited exceptions for serious offenses involving weapons, drugs, or serious bodily injury.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>What Happens if the Behavior is NOT a "Manifestation"?</b> If the team determines that the behavior *is not* a manifestation of the student's disability, the student may be subject to the same disciplinary measures as students without disabilities. However, the school district MUST continue to provide FAPE (Free Appropriate Public Education) during any period of suspension. This may involve providing services in an interim alternative educational setting.</p>

  <p><b>Parent Advocacy Tip:</b> Prior to the MDR meeting, thoroughly review your child's IEP, any behavior plans, and all relevant documentation. Prepare a statement outlining your perspective on the behavior and its relationship to your child's disability. Gather any relevant medical or psychological information that supports your position. Bring a trusted advocate or attorney to the MDR meeting for support.</p>

    <p><b>District 10 Specific Considerations:</b> Familiarize yourself with District 10's policies regarding MDR procedures. Request copies of any relevant district guidelines or protocols in advance of the meeting.</p>

  <h3>Navigating Suspensions in District 10: Know Your Rights</h3>
  <p>If your child is facing suspension in District 10, it's essential to understand your rights and the procedures involved. Suspensions can be either short-term (10 days or less) or long-term (more than 10 days).</p>

    <p><b>Short-Term Suspensions (10 days or less):</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Right to Notification:</b> You have the right to be notified of the charges against your child, including the specific misconduct alleged.</li>
        <li><b>Right to Explanation of Evidence:</b> The school must provide an explanation of the evidence supporting the charges.</li>
        <li><b>Right to be Heard:</b> Your child has the right to present their side of the story to the principal or other school official. This can be in person or in writing.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>Long-Term Suspensions (more than 10 days):</b> These suspensions trigger the MDR process (if the student has an IEP or 504 Plan) and require a formal Superintendent's Suspension Hearing.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Right to a Formal Hearing:</b>  You have the right to a formal hearing before the Superintendent or their designee.</li>
        <li><b>Right to Counsel:</b> You have the right to be represented by an attorney or advocate at the hearing.</li>
        <li><b>Right to Present Evidence:</b> You have the right to present evidence and witnesses on your child's behalf.</li>
        <li><b>Right to Cross-Examine Witnesses:</b> You have the right to cross-examine witnesses presented by the school district.</li>
        <li><b>Right to a Written Decision:</b> You have the right to a written decision from the Superintendent outlining the findings of fact and the reasons for the suspension.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>Step-by-Step Guide to a Superintendent's Suspension Hearing in District 10:</b></p>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Receive Notice:</b> You will receive a notice of the suspension and the date, time, and location of the Superintendent's Suspension Hearing. Review the notice carefully and note all deadlines.</li>
        <li><b>Gather Information:</b> Gather all relevant documentation related to the incident, including incident reports, witness statements, and your child's school records.</li>
        <li><b>Prepare Your Case:</b> Prepare a statement outlining your child's perspective on the incident. Identify any mitigating circumstances. Gather character references or supporting documentation. If your child has an IEP or 504 Plan, ensure you are prepared to discuss its relevance.</li>
        <li><b>Consider Legal Representation:</b> Consult with an attorney or advocate specializing in special education law. They can provide valuable guidance and represent you at the hearing. Legal Aid Society and other organizations offer free or low-cost legal services to eligible families.</li>
        <li><b>Attend the Hearing:</b> Arrive at the hearing prepared to present your case in a clear and concise manner. Be respectful and professional.</li>
        <li><b>Appeal (If Necessary):</b> If you disagree with the Superintendent's decision, you have the right to appeal to the New York State Commissioner of Education within 30 days of the decision.</li>
    </ol>

  <p><b>Parent Advocacy Tips for Suspension Hearings:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Document Everything:</b> Keep detailed records of all communication with the school district, including dates, times, and the names of individuals you spoke with.</li>
        <li><b>Request Access to Records:</b> Request access to your child's school records, including disciplinary records, attendance records, and academic records.</li>
        <li><b>Highlight Mitigating Circumstances:</b> Emphasize any factors that may have contributed to your child's behavior, such as emotional distress, bullying, or a lack of support from the school.</li>
        <li><b>Focus on Positive Behavior:</b> Highlight your child's positive qualities and achievements.</li>
        <li><b>Advocate for Alternative Solutions:</b> Suggest alternative disciplinary measures, such as counseling, restorative justice, or a change in placement.</li>
        <li><b>Understand the Appeal Process:</b> Familiarize yourself with the procedures for appealing a suspension decision to the New York State Commissioner of Education.  The appeal process is governed by Section 3214 of the Education Law.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>Common District 10 Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Failure to Conduct Timely MDRs:</b> Ensure that the district adheres to the timeline for conducting MDRs (within 10 school days of the disciplinary action). Document any delays and request written explanations.</li>
        <li><b>Inadequate IEP Implementation:</b> If the district failed to implement your child's IEP, argue that the behavior was a direct result of this failure. Present evidence of the IEP's requirements and the district's non-compliance.</li>
        <li><b>Lack of Behavioral Supports:</b> If your child's behavior is related to their disability, advocate for appropriate behavioral supports, such as a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA) and a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP).</li>
        <li><b>Insufficient Notice of Hearings:</b> Ensure that you receive adequate notice of suspension hearings, including the specific charges against your child and the evidence supporting those charges.</li>
        <li><b>Ignoring Parent Input:</b> Actively participate in all disciplinary proceedings and ensure that your input is considered. Document any instances where your concerns are dismissed or ignored.</li>
    </ul>

  <h3>Due Process Rights Under IDEA</h3>

    <p>If you disagree with the school district's decisions regarding your child's discipline, you have the right to file a due process complaint under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA). Due process is a formal legal process that allows you to resolve disputes with the school district.</p>

    <p><b>Grounds for Filing a Due Process Complaint:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Disagreement with the MDR determination.</li>
        <li>Failure to implement the IEP.</li>
        <li>Inappropriate disciplinary measures.</li>
        <li>Denial of FAPE (Free Appropriate Public Education).</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>The Due Process Process:</b></p>
    <ol>
        <li><b>File a Complaint:</b> You must file a written due process complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED) and provide a copy to the school district. The complaint must include specific information about the dispute and the relief you are seeking.</li>
        <li><b>Resolution Meeting:</b> The school district must convene a resolution meeting with you within 15 days of receiving the complaint. The purpose of the meeting is to attempt to resolve the dispute.</li>
        <li><b>Mediation (Optional):</b> You may choose to participate in mediation with the school district. Mediation is a voluntary process that allows you to work with a neutral third party to reach a settlement.</li>
        <li><b>Due Process Hearing:</b> If the dispute is not resolved through the resolution meeting or mediation, you have the right to a due process hearing before an impartial hearing officer.</li>
        <li><b>Hearing Officer Decision:</b> The hearing officer will issue a written decision within 45 days of the hearing.</li>
        <li><b>Appeal (Optional):</b> If you disagree with the hearing officer's decision, you have the right to appeal to a State Review Officer (SRO).</li>
    </ol>

    <p><b>Statute of Limitations:</b>  There is a statute of limitations on filing a due process complaint. You must file the complaint within two years of the date you knew or should have known about the alleged violation. 34 CFR § 300.507.</p>

    <p><b>Resources for Due Process:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>NYSED Office of Special Education:</b> Provides information and resources on due process rights and procedures.</li>
        <li><b>Special Education Attorneys and Advocates:</b> Can provide legal representation and advocacy services.</li>
    </ul>

  <h3>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h3>

    <dl>
        <dt><b>Q: What is the difference between a suspension and an expulsion?</b></dt>
        <dd>A suspension is a temporary removal of a student from school. An expulsion is a permanent removal of a student from school. Expulsions are rare in New York State, particularly for students with disabilities.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: Can my child be suspended for behavior that occurs off-campus?</b></dt>
        <dd>Yes, in some cases. According to NYSED guidelines, a student can be disciplined for off-campus behavior if it disrupts the educational environment or poses a threat to the safety of students or staff.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: What is an Interim Alternative Educational Setting (IAES)?</b></dt>
        <dd>An IAES is a temporary placement for a student who has been suspended or expelled. The IAES must provide the student with FAPE and allow the student to continue to participate in the general education curriculum, to the extent possible. 34 CFR § 300.530(c).</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: Can the school district require me to medicate my child as a condition of returning to school?</b></dt>
        <dd>No. The school district cannot require you to medicate your child. Medication decisions are made by you and your child's doctor.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: What if my child is being bullied?</b></dt>
        <dd>Report the bullying to the school immediately. The school district is required to investigate and take steps to stop the bullying. If the bullying is related to your child's disability, it may be a violation of IDEA.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: Where can I find the New York State Part 200 Regulations?</b></dt>
        <dd>You can find the New York State Part 200 Regulations on the New York State Education Department (NYSED) website. Search for "New York State Part 200 Regulations."</dd>
    </dl>

</body>
